RGB-D semantic segmentation benefits from complementary appearance and geometry, but treating HHA as an ordinary three-channel image ignores its physical heterogeneity and can propagate corrupted geometry into RGB semantics. We present RSGNet, an asymmetric framework that keeps RGB as the only semantic backbone and introduces Physics-Factorized HHA Routing (PFHR). PFHR first canonicalizes HHA to disparity-height-angle order and encodes the three channels independently. A multiplicative disparity-height relation produces a low-frequency layout prompt, while reliability-conditioned high-frequency responses from all three channels produce a boundary prompt; the angle channel is represented by sine and cosine orientation components. Separate layout and boundary reliability proxies are trained with controlled degradation. A spatial stage router preserves both prompts until injection, favors boundary evidence at high-resolution stages and layout evidence at deep stages, and applies reliability once to the routed residual. RSGNet combines local pixel interaction, an economical intermediate adapter, linear-complexity deep semantic mixing, identity-preserving cross-scale residuals, and a fixed lightweight decoder. Parameter-matched raw-depth, unified-HHA, independent-channel, correct-route, and swapped-route controls make the physical factorization directly testable under a fixed 100-epoch reporting protocol.
